  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAs a low pressure system continues to drift toward the Northeast US \ntoday, high pressure is expected to build southeast across the area \nand allow for dry and pleasant conditions today and tomorrow with \nhighs climbing into the upper 70s to low 80s and sunny skies. This \nhigh pressure will drift off the Mid-Atlantic Coast by Wednesday as \na weak low pressure and mid-level shortwave push northeast along the \nAppalachian Mountains. This disturbance will allow for the return of \nshowers and possible thunderstorms along and east of I-71. Given the \npositioning of the low, much of the area may remain dry until a \nbroad upper level trough begins to impact the region on Thursday. \n\nOn Thursday, the aforementioned trough will begin to advect mid-\nlevel energy into the region as it moves a surface cold front east. \nModels suggest this cold front stalling somewhere near the central \nGreat Lakes region and acting as a path for an additional surface \nlow to move from the Plains into the region. Additionally, increase \nmoisture and WAA should assist with destabilization across the area \non Thursday presenting the potential for thunderstorms during the \nafternoon and into the overnight hours. Another round of convection \nis possible again on Friday as the aforementioned boundary slowly \nmeanders east. Will have to keep an eye on this boundary as current \nmodels suggest that the positioning of the mean flow could result in \nthe training of some storms. In addition, given the spread amongst \nmodels, there is low confidence in the severe potential, but will \nneed to keep an eye on it given the favorable synoptic setup. \nTemperatures throughout the week are expected to remain near normal \nwith highs in the 80s and overnight lows in the 60s.",
